const FIXES=[
['Hebrew sheet: likely typos','A few spellings on the United Hatzalah sheet look off: Skin Color is written צבע אור ("light colour"), should be צבע עור. Secondary Assessment סבבת שניוני is usually סבב שניוני. Gunshot Wound פצועה ירי should be פציעת ירי. In Quality, סוחת should be סוחט (squeezing). Single-use razor יד פעמי should be חד פעמי. ACS is transliterated "Chamoora" but the Hebrew חריפה reads "Charifa". The study center shows the sheet as written; learn the corrected forms.'],
['Glucose emergencies are written backwards','Your assessment doc says "Hyperglycemic emergency – <400" and "Hypoglycemic emergency – >60". The signs are flipped. It should be hyperglycemic above about 400 and hypoglycemic below about 60. This one is worth fixing today: it\'s the kind of thing an exam question is built on.'],
['Oral glucose needs a safe airway','Add the condition: give oral glucose only if the patient is awake enough to swallow and protect their own airway. An unresponsive or seizing patient gets nothing by mouth.'],
['"Unresponsive" doesn\'t guarantee no gag reflex','Your AVPU line defines U as having no gag or cough reflex. Some unresponsive patients still gag, which is exactly why you watch for gagging when inserting an OPA.'],
['Head-trauma warning is on the wrong adjunct','Your OPA section says to avoid it in head trauma because it may hit the skull. That risk belongs to the NPA: with a basilar skull fracture, an NPA can go through the fractured bone. The OPA modification for head injury in your notes (no rotation) is your course\'s rule; the textbook rule is "no NPA with suspected skull or facial fracture."'],
['Oxygen formula is missing the safe residual','The textbook formula subtracts a 200 psi safe residual: (gauge psi − 200) × constant ÷ LPM. Without it you overestimate how long the tank lasts.'],
['Suction limits for kids and infants','You wrote 15 seconds for adults. Add: 10 seconds for children and 5 seconds for infants.'],
['Anaphylaxis needs a treatment line','The note lists causes and hives only. Add: airway swelling, wheezing, hypotension, and the treatment: epinephrine auto-injector per protocol, high-flow O₂, ALS, treat for shock.'],
['NPA sizing differs from AAOS','AAOS measures the NPA from the tip of the nose to the earlobe. Your notes say angle of the jaw. Ask your instructor which one your skills exam expects.'],
['Rigid vs soft catheter','Your notes tie rigid catheters to patients who can\'t be turned. In AAOS terms: rigid (tonsil tip) for the mouth and thick material, soft (French) for the nose and places a rigid tip can\'t reach. Keep your course rule if your instructor taught it that way.'],
['Shock stages are missing','Add compensated (BP normal, tachycardia, cool clammy skin) → decompensated (BP falling, a late sign) → irreversible. It shows up on almost every exam.'],
['Shut-off wording','"Main valve first, then flowmeter" is right. Add the step between: let the regulator bleed until the gauge reads zero, then turn the flowmeter off.'],
['Shock care: bleeding control first','Your treatment list starts with positioning. For trauma, controlling external bleeding comes before everything else. IV access is ALS scope in most systems, not BLS.'],
['OPA colour code','"Size 9 / orange" depends on the manufacturer. Sizing by measurement is what the exam tests.']];

